    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The idea of a hybrid approach is helpful, but I'm worried about the long-term maintenance. If we don't have a perfect system from day one, there's a real danger that the collection will just become a disorganized mess over time as new items are added or misplaced. What are the specific risks of \"classification drift\" where the collection loses its logical structure and starts to feel more like a heap of unmanaged items than a curated archive?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Classification drift occurs when the underlying cataloging rules become inconsistently applied across different items, leading to a loss of discoverability. The primary risk is that new acquisitions might be classified using ad-hoc methods, creating \"island\" entries that are logically orphaned from the main structure. To mitigate this, establish rigorous, mandated metadata standards and conduct regular collection audits, ensuring that any new cataloger adheres strictly to the agreed-upon hybrid framework for consistency.",
      "source": "target",
